The statement is **true**. Here is the proof.

---

**Theorem.** If a CW complex $X$ is $(n-1)$-connected ($n \ge 0$), then its suspension $SX$ is $n$-connected.

**Recall.** A space $Y$ is $k$-connected if $\pi_i(Y)=0$ for all $0\le i\le k$ (with $(-1)$-connected meaning nonempty). The suspension is $SX=(X\times[-1,1])/(X\times\{-1\}\sim *,\; X\times\{1\}\sim *)$, the union of two cones $C_+X\cup C_-X$ with $C_+X\cap C_-X\simeq X$.

**Key fact (suspension shifts reduced homology).** For any nonempty CW complex $X$,
$$\widetilde H_k(SX)\cong \widetilde H_{k-1}(X),\qquad k\ge 0.$$
*Proof.* Apply the reduced Mayer–Vietoris sequence to $SX=C_+X\cup C_-X$: both cones are contractible, so the sequence gives $\widetilde H_k(SX)\cong \widetilde H_{k-1}(C_+X\cap C_-X)\cong \widetilde H_{k-1}(X)$. $\square$

---

**Case $n=0$.** $X$ is $(-1)$-connected, i.e. nonempty. Then $SX$ is path-connected (every point lies in a cone over $X$, hence connects to an apex). So $SX$ is $0$-connected. $\checkmark$

**Case $n=1$.** $X$ is $0$-connected (path-connected). Since $SX=C_+X\cup C_-X$ with both cones contractible and $C_+X\cap C_-X\simeq X$ path-connected, van Kampen gives $\pi_1(SX)\cong \pi_1(C_+X)*_{\pi_1(X)}\pi_1(C_-X)=1*_{\pi_1(X)}1=1$. Also $SX$ is path-connected, so $SX$ is $1$-connected. $\checkmark$

**Case $n\ge 2$.** Here $X$ is $(n-1)$-connected with $n-1\ge 1$, so $X$ is path-connected. As in the $n=1$ case, $SX$ is simply connected, i.e. $1$-connected.

Since $X$ is $(n-1)$-connected, by the Hurewicz theorem (with $k=n\ge 2$) we have $\widetilde H_i(X)=0$ for $1\le i\le n-1$. Combined with path-connectedness ($\widetilde H_0(X)=0$), this gives
$$\widetilde H_i(X)=0\quad\text{for }0\le i\le n-1.$$
By the suspension-shifts-homology fact,
$$\widetilde H_i(SX)\cong \widetilde H_{i-1}(X)=0\quad\text{for }1\le i\le n,$$
and $\widetilde H_0(SX)=0$ since $SX$ is path-connected. Hence
$$\widetilde H_i(SX)=0\quad\text{for }0\le i\le n.$$

Now apply Hurewicz to $SX$ inductively. We know $SX$ is $1$-connected. Suppose $SX$ is $(m-1)$-connected for some $2\le m\le n$. Hurewicz gives $\pi_m(SX)\cong H_m(SX)=\widetilde H_m(SX)=0$, so $SX$ is $m$-connected. By induction from $m=2$ up to $m=n$, we conclude $\pi_i(SX)=0$ for $0\le i\le n$.

Therefore $SX$ is $n$-connected. $\checkmark$

---

All cases are covered, so the statement holds for every $n\ge 0$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
